WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.584 --> 00:00:02.127
Tetris,

00:00:03.253 --> 00:00:04.212
It’s the perfect game.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:05.880
I’m going to go to Moscow,

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:07.757
secure worldwide handheld rights.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.386
You’re walking into a communist country

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:14.556
that still considers America enemy numero uno.

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:16.891
If you go, we can’t protect you.

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:18.893
Okie dokie.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:23.648
The movie is an extraordinary story

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:26.484
set in Soviet Russia in 1989…

00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:28.445
about different people from all over the world

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:30.947
 pursuing this incredibly hot property.

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:32.282
My character Henk

00:00:32.282 --> 00:00:34.951
is a kind of larger than life cowboy figure

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:36.536
that goes out on a limb to make his fortune

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:37.787
and get the rights to Tetris.

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:40.081
When I first found Tetris I was a publisher of

00:00:40.081 --> 00:00:41.499
computer games in Japan,

00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:43.835
the people who were publishing it had no idea what they had,

00:00:43.835 --> 00:00:45.545
I got the console rights in Japan,

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:46.963
and then Game Boy came out.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:49.090
Gentlemen if you want to sell a couple hundred thousand

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.634
Game Boys to little kids, package it with Mario.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.971
But if you want to sell millions of Game Boys...

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.142
package it with Tetris.

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:01.561
I made a deal with the president of Nintendo USA,

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:03.646
a month later I was on a plane to Moscow to get

00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:04.564
the Game Boy rights

00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:08.276
We’re in a room full of eight business people,

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.195
there were definitely some KGB guys in the room.

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:11.446
You go home today,

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:13.740
or you stay and face the consequences.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.952
Alexey Pajitnov and I first met in that room.

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:19.913
He’s the original creator of Tetris.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.415
Henk was the first man

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.459
Alexey can talk about computers to.

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:26.628
Alexey, your game is brilliant,

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:27.837
I'm going to make you a millionaire.

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:30.799
I do not have right to receive money from my game.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:31.674
That’s criminal.

00:01:31.674 --> 00:01:32.175
No,

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:34.511
it is communism.

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:38.181
Tetris is my main achievement in my life.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.267
It was the only Russian software

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.895
coming out of Soviet Union at that time.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.105
There’s lots of corruption there

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:47.857
and people after money and there's this one man

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:49.442
and people after money and there's this one man

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.486
who’s made this thing that everyone wants. 

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.739
We’ll start by licensing computer games,

00:01:54.739 --> 00:01:59.786
then video games, arcade games, board games, everything.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.372
It’s this mad dash,

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:04.791
to secure

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:07.293
the rights to the game.

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:11.339
Get me Tetris.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:14.968
Don’t sacrifice everything for this.

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:15.969
Get in!

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.016
We were living in this two bedroom apartment

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:24.227
supporting this crazy foreigner father,

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:28.439
it’s really a transformational time in our family's lives.

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:30.191
Everything is going to be fine,

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:32.735
because if it isn’t, then we don’t have handheld rights

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:35.572
and we don’t have console rights, and Nintendo will sue us

00:02:35.572 --> 00:02:38.533
for fraud and we’ll lose the business and they’ll probably

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:41.536
take our house and I am trying to keep it together

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:44.038
Akemi, really I am but I do not have a backup plan.

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:48.126
It’s strange watching somebody else be me.

00:02:48.126 --> 00:02:50.753
It was fun to watch because he does a good job.

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:53.381
I was impressed with the script

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:54.507
and now the actors as well,

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.384
they really captured what the real people were like

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:00.638
Personally for my part I always just feel that you

00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:03.766
seek to humanize somebody and make them relatable

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:05.268
and three dimensional, then you are

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:07.103
doing them the right service.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:08.354
I’m sorry hun,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.316
Papa’s got to go and get ready for his business trip,

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:14.444
but I can’t wait to see your show.

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:18.323
Tetris crosses boundaries,

00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:21.075
you know it brings people of all cultures together.

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:23.202
Henk &amp; Alexey they’re incredibly different

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:26.706
in terms of their family lives but they love programming.

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:30.460
Partners are what make us great.

00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:33.421
Not only is Tetris an amazing game,

00:03:33.421 --> 00:03:36.341
but it was only possible through this friendship between

00:03:36.341 --> 00:03:39.510
Henk &amp; Alexey that it spread through the rest of the world.

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:41.679
He is the author of Tetris.

00:03:43.097 --> 00:03:45.099
play Tetris,

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:46.392
my friends.

00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:48.436
When you take a photo,

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:51.314
how do you make the photo have the same emotional impact

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:52.941
as when you were actually there.

00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:56.819
I think the movie does that very well.

